course: dictionary
lesson: 1091
title: Brackish water
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Water on Earth is not all the same. Fresh water, like a river or a pond, has hardly any salt. Sea water is very salty. Brackish water is in between: a mix of the two, slightly salty.
explain: Brackish water is often found where a river meets the sea, in places like estuaries, lagoons and marshes. Special plants and animals live there, because they can cope with water that is not fully fresh and not fully salty.
explain: You can use the context to work out the word. «The Baltic Sea is brackish, much less salty than the ocean, because many rivers pour fresh water into it.» The words "much less salty than the ocean" and the rivers pouring in tell you that brackish means salty, but not as salty as the sea.
explain: The word can also mean water that is a bit salty or dirty and not nice to drink. So if a text says a pond is brackish, you know not to drink from it.
fact: Brackish means slightly salty, a mix of fresh water and sea water.
fact: «Brackish» is a word used for estuaries, mangroves, marshes and lagoons.
fact: Brackish water is often found where a river meets the sea.
fact: «The Baltic Sea is brackish» because many rivers pour fresh water into it, so it is much less salty than the ocean.
fact: Brackish water can also be water that is a bit salty or dirty, so it is not nice to drink.
exercise: What does «brackish» mean? | Very hot and dry like a desert | Deep, cold and dark | *Slightly salty, a mix of fresh and sea water | Completely fresh, with no salt at all
exercise: In «The Baltic Sea is brackish, much less salty than the ocean», which words give you the clue about how salty it is? | brackish is | *much less salty than the ocean | The Baltic Sea | the ocean
exercise: Where would you most likely find brackish water? | *An estuary, where a river meets the sea | A high mountain lake covered in snow | A dry desert with sand dunes | A swimming pool with chlorine
test: Brackish water is a mix of which two kinds of water? | Hot and cold water | Rain and snow | *Fresh water and sea water | Ice and steam
test: Why might special plants and animals live in brackish lagoons? | They need boiling hot water | They are plants that only grow in fully fresh water | They only live in deep, dark water | *They can cope with water that is not fully fresh and not fully salty
exam: Which best explains «brackish» in «The water in the pond was brackish, so we did not drink it»? | *Slightly salty or dirty, so not nice to drink | Full of fizzy bubbles | Very cold and icy | Crystal clear and sparkling
